00:43:53,972 --> 00:43:57,118


315
00:43:57,138 --> 00:43:58,220


316
00:44:06,671 --> 00:44:07,612


317
00:44:34,140 --> 00:44:40,582
The nine mental placements, I won't go into a great detail on them, but essentially the nine are as follows.

318
00:44:42,103 --> 00:44:49,426
Resting of mind, where one rests one's attention upon the image, in this case the image of the Buddha.

319
00:44:50,746 --> 00:44:55,608
And then there is continual placement or resting upon that image.

320
00:44:56,369 --> 00:45:01,231
And then there is continuous resettling of the mind upon the image.

321
00:45:02,291 --> 00:45:13,436
What this means is that in the early stages of practice, one will need to continually place one's mind on the image as one comes under the influence of distraction.

322
00:45:14,216 --> 00:45:17,678
Nevertheless, through familiarity of replacing one's attention

323
00:45:19,239 --> 00:45:36,288
the chosen image then distraction will become less and less then one will fully be able to settle one's mind that is the fourth mental placement fully settling here one is able to as the name dictates fully settle upon the image of the Buddha

324
00:45:36,648 --> 00:45:51,879
Thereafter, one passes through the fifth mental placement of taming, the sixth of pacifying, the seventh of complete pacification, the eighth of one-pointedness of mind, and then the ninth where one rests in equanimity.

325
00:45:52,420 --> 00:45:55,142
Essentially, with these mental placements,

326
00:45:57,704 --> 00:46:08,402
one refines one's attention, one's attention that is single-pointedly focused upon the chosen image, in this case the form of the Buddha.

327
00:46:11,312 --> 00:46:14,933
.

